<TopicPage slug="subjects/film/intermediate/editing-the-cut">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The cut is <strong>cinema\'s signature power</strong> — meaning created in the instant between
    two frames, available to no other art. Editing is where a film is written the final time:
    structure, rhythm and performance are chosen at the timeline.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="invisibility, montage, rhythm, the modern cut" widget="equationStep" config={cutCfg} />

  <h2>Craft knowledge</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The edit point is emotional:</strong> cut on the look before the reaction, not the reaction itself — leaving space for the audience\'s anticipation is more powerful than showing the payoff (Walter Murch\'s Rule of Six: emotion outranks story, rhythm, eyeline, 2-D and 3-D continuity).</li>
    <li><strong>Kuleshov in the editing suite:</strong> the effect is an editor\'s everyday tool — the same neutral shot reads differently by its neighbours (the grammar lesson\'s founding experiment, used industrially).</li>
    <li><strong>Skip the dialogue:</strong> scenes cut tighter than the script read; good editors find the scene inside the coverage — "the film is written three times: script, set, edit."</li>
    <li><strong>Sound bridges the cut:</strong> hearing the next scene before seeing it smooths the join (J-cuts) or deliberately lurches it (L-cuts held for discomfort) — the sound lesson\'s territory, where the next lesson continues.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Murch, In the Blink of an Eye; Eisenstein, Film Form; the grammar lesson\'s Kuleshov section.</p>
</TopicPage>
